/* Auto-generated full-text search index. Rebuild via the index script. */
window.DX_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"/docs","p":"Welcome","g":"Moony Documentation","s":"What is Moony","a":"what","t":"Moony (MNY) is sound digital money. Its supply is fixed forever at 21,000,000 coins, governed by a single immutable program, so no issuer can inflate it, no operator can freeze or pause it, and no one holds privileged access. It is permissionless, trustless, and censorship-resistant by design: anyone, anywhere can hold it, send it, and spend it like cash, without asking permission. consts.rs · supply"},{"f":"/docs","p":"Welcome","g":"Moony Documentation","s":"Open by design","a":"open","t":"Moony is built to be a digital public good: open, permissionless, and neutral. Anyone can hold it, transact with it, audit it, or build on top of it without asking permission. It was created by Moony Labs, LLC on open-source infrastructure, the Flipcash Reserve Contract, MIT-licensed by Code Inc. and audited by Sec3. Moony Labs deployed a single currency on that standard, named it Moony, and then relinquished control entirely: no keys, no admin authority, no ability to intervene. That hands-off position is deliberate, an act of decentralization, not an absence. Moony runs as neutral public infrastructure, shaped by open participation rather than any company."},{"f":"/docs","p":"Welcome","g":"Moony Documentation","s":"Explore the docs","a":"explore","t":"Each section stands on its own, start wherever you need. Acquire, query, and redeem Moony from the open-source CLI, or build your own interface against the program directly. The asset, the Reserve, and the deterministic pricing curve that governs it. The independent participants who grow Moony, and the incentives that align them. Permissionless payments, micropayments, and composable capital markets."},{"f":"/docs","p":"Welcome","g":"Moony Documentation","s":"","a":"","t":"A fixed-supply digital currency, distributed and priced by an immutable program on Solana. This is the complete protocol reference — start anywhere."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/overview","p":"Overview","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"","a":"protocol","t":"Moony is an open currency: a digital public good governed by a decentralized, immutable monetary protocol. It provides open access to financial participation, enables trustless peer-to-peer value exchange, and establishes a composable foundation for a decentralized global economy. At the core of the protocol is an immutable program on the Solana blockchain that deterministically governs the distribution and circulation of a fixed-supply digital asset, without discretionary control or centralized coordination. Deterministic by design. Immutable by default. Neutral by principle."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/overview","p":"Overview","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Design principles","a":"principles","t":"Five properties define what Moony is and what it refuses to be. Each is a direct consequence of the protocol's design, not a policy that can be revised. Permissionless Anyone can acquire, hold, send, or build on Moony without approval, identity verification, or minimum balance. There is no gatekeeper to grant or deny access. Trustless Distribution and pricing are executed by an immutable program, not by a counterparty. You verify the rules rather than trust an operator to honor them. Censorship-resistant Valid transactions settle regardless of who sends them or why. No authority can selectively block, freeze, or reverse a transfer. Fixed supply 21,000,000 MNY, set at genesis and enforced forever. The mint authority is revoked; no issuance, dilution, or debasement is possible. Immutable & open The program is MIT-licensed, audited, and unchangeable. Its logic is public, its behavior is deterministic, and anyone can build a compatible interface."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/overview","p":"Overview","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Protocol Components","a":"components","t":"Fixed-supply digital currency built for payments. Immutable program governing distribution and pricing. Deterministic pricing as a function of circulating supply."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/overview","p":"Overview","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"","a":"","t":"The open monetary protocol that governs Moony, designed as a digital public good."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/mny","p":"Moony (MNY)","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"","a":"mny","t":"Moony (MNY) is a digital currency on Solana built for permissionless peer-to-peer payments without intermediaries. It is the foundational unit of exchange for an open, composable economy."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/mny","p":"Moony (MNY)","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Token Addresses","a":"addresses","t":"MNY Token Mint MNY Update Authority"},{"f":"/docs/protocol/mny","p":"Moony (MNY)","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Fixed Supply","a":"fixed-supply","t":"Moony has a fixed maximum supply of 21 million units, an immutable constraint that cryptographically ensures the protocol's long-term economic integrity. This cap mirrors Bitcoin's supply design, reflecting a shared commitment to absolute digital scarcity as a foundation for sound money. There is no mechanism by which additional units can ever be created. The mint authority has been permanently revoked, with no administrative keys, governance votes, or protocol upgrades able to expand the supply beyond the fixed cap. consts.rs · supply Absolute Scarcity There will never be more than 21 million Moony in circulation. Scarcity is not a policy. It is a permanent property of sound money. No Inflation Unlike fiat currencies that lose purchasing power as supply expands, Moony's supply is fixed. It cannot be diluted or debased. Code-Enforced No entity, mechanism, or protocol upgrade can alter Moony's monetary rules. The mint authority has been permanently revoked and the supply constraint is publicly verifiable by anyone onchain."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/mny","p":"Moony (MNY)","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Core Properties","a":"core-properties","t":"MNY is designed to function as permissionless digital cash, with transaction properties that support direct ownership, voluntary self-custody, and censorship-resistant exchange. These characteristics enable open participation in economic activity, extending access to anyone, anywhere, on equal terms. Permissionless Transactions do not require approval from centralized authorities. Anyone can send or receive MNY without identity verification, minimum balances, or institutional gatekeeping. Censorship Resistant Transfers cannot be selectively blocked or filtered by third parties. Valid transactions are processed by the network regardless of the parties involved or the nature of the exchange. Irreversible Once confirmed onchain, transactions are final and cannot be reversed, modified, or disputed. This provides settlement certainty without reliance on intermediaries or escrow. Self-Custody Participants hold and control their own assets without surrendering custody to banks or intermediaries. Value can move as freely as information across the network."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/mny","p":"Moony (MNY)","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Divisibility","a":"divisibility","t":"MNY is divisible to 10 decimal places, enabling transactions as small as 0.0000000001 MNY. Combined with Solana's sub-cent transaction fees, this precision opens economic activity at scales where traditional payment rails are not viable."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/mny","p":"Moony (MNY)","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"","a":"","t":"The native digital asset of the Moony protocol."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/reserve","p":"The Reserve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"","a":"reserve","t":"The Reserve is the trustless mechanism through which all MNY enters circulation, exclusively in exchange for verifiable onchain capital. Rather than relying on discretionary allocation, private sales, or centralized control, the entire MNY supply is held in a program that releases tokens into circulation according to transparent, immutable rules. This ensures that every unit of MNY is acquired on equal terms, with no special access or preferential treatment. Deposited capital remains onchain as redemption liquidity. Every unit of MNY in circulation corresponds to capital held in the Reserve, and the ability to redeem at price levels determined by the pricing curve is a mathematical invariant of the system. This creates a persistent, verifiable liquidity foundation that grows with the network."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/reserve","p":"The Reserve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Reserve Addresses","a":"addresses","t":"Reserve Account MNY Vault USDF Vault"},{"f":"/docs/protocol/reserve","p":"The Reserve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"How It Works","a":"how-it-works","t":"All MNY enters and exits circulation through the Reserve. Both buying and selling execute atomically onchain, requiring no intermediaries, approvals, or custodial trust. Deposit USDF into the Reserve to acquire MNY. The amount received is set by the current position on the pricing curve. As more MNY enters circulation, the price rises along the curve. Return MNY to the Reserve to redeem USDF at the current curve price, less a 1% fee. Redemption liquidity is a mathematical invariant, maintained by the capital held in the Reserve. The Reserve's entire state lives in one onchain pool account. Anyone can read it (the get-currency command does exactly this) and verify the vaults, accrued fees, and fee rate directly. pool.rs · state"},{"f":"/docs/protocol/reserve","p":"The Reserve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Fee Model","a":"fee-model","t":"There is no fee to acquire MNY from the Reserve. Redemptions incur a 1% fee, deducted from the USDF returned to the participant. This fee is hardcoded in the program and applies uniformly to all redemptions regardless of size or participant. consts.rs · fees Security The fee creates deliberate economic friction on sell-side transactions, making sandwich attacks and other MEV extraction strategies unprofitable against the pricing curve. Fee handling Collected fees accrue onchain as fees_accumulated and can be burned by the Pool's authority, Flipcash Inc., which built and maintains the open-source platform Moony was created on. The rate is fixed in code and applies identically to every redemption."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/reserve","p":"The Reserve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Reserve Asset: USDF","a":"usdf","t":"USDF is a dollar-denominated stablecoin that serves as the Reserve's unit of account. Issued in partnership between Flipcash and Coinbase as a Coinbase Custom Stablecoin, each USDF is fully backed 1:1 by U.S. dollar-denominated collateral held in segregated Coinbase custody. USDF Token Mint While Flipcash and Coinbase manage USDF issuance and custody, neither controls the Moony protocol. All pricing, distribution, and redemption behavior is governed solely by immutable onchain logic."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/reserve","p":"The Reserve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Open-Source Infrastructure","a":"open-source","t":"The Reserve program was developed and open-sourced by Code Inc. under the MIT License. Moony Labs used it to create the Moony currency, minting the fixed supply and initializing its Reserve onchain, then relinquished all control. Neither Code Inc. nor Moony Labs can alter the protocol; all distribution, pricing, and redemption behavior is governed exclusively by immutable onchain logic that cannot be changed by any party. This architecture makes Moony fully open and composable. Developers can integrate MNY across wallets, applications, and protocols without permission or coordination. MIT License The underlying program infrastructure is released under the MIT License, a permissive open-source license that allows unrestricted use, modification, and distribution. Security Audit The program infrastructure has been independently audited by Sec3, a security firm specializing in Solana program analysis. The audit was completed in January 2026 with all findings resolved. The full report is publicly available."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/reserve","p":"The Reserve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"","a":"","t":"The trustless mechanism through which all MNY enters circulation."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/pricing-curve","p":"Pricing Curve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"","a":"curve","t":"Moony's primary price discovery mechanism is an onchain pricing curve, a deterministic exponential function encoded within the Reserve program. Unlike traditional market-based price discovery that relies on order books or liquidity pools, the pricing curve calculates the price of MNY based solely on the current circulating supply. As tokens enter circulation through acquisition from the Reserve, the price rises along a mathematically defined trajectory. As tokens are redeemed and removed from circulation, the price falls along the same curve in reverse."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/pricing-curve","p":"Pricing Curve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Explore the curve","a":"explore-curve","t":"Drag to set circulating supply and watch the spot price move along the curve. These are the live onchain constants, computed in real time. No presale, no insider allocation. All 21M MNY begin in the Reserve and enter circulation only as capital flows in. The first coin sold for $0.01; the price rises about $0.01 for roughly every $11,400 added, up to $1,000,000 for the final coin. The same curve, for everyone. That $1.14 trillion total is the definite integral of the pricing curve across the entire 0–21M range, encoded onchain as a protocol constant."},{"f":"/docs/protocol/pricing-curve","p":"Pricing Curve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Spot Price","a":"spot-price","t":"The spot price represents the instantaneous price per MNY token at any given circulating supply level. It is a continuous mathematical function with no bid-ask spread and no dependence on external liquidity. It is defined by an exponential equation with three immutable constants: P(S), spot price in USDF at supply level S S, current circulating supply of MNY tokens e, Euler's number (~2.71828), the base of the natural exponential function The exponential form ensures that the rate of price change is proportional to the current price itself, creating smooth, continuous price adjustment without discontinuities at any supply level. curve.rs · spot_price_at_supply Three immutable constants parameterize the curve to produce an initial price of ~0.01 USDF and a terminal price of 1,000,000 USDF at maximum supply (21M MNY): a = 11400.230149967394933471 Amplitude constant. Scales the curve to reach $1,000,000 USDF at maximum supply. b = 0.000000877175273521 Initial price coefficient. Sets the starting price at a × b ≈ $0.01 USDF when supply is near zero. c = 0.000000877175273521 Growth rate. Controls how rapidly price increases with supply. Note: c = b by design, which simplifies integral c"},{"f":"/docs/protocol/pricing-curve","p":"Pricing Curve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Transaction Formulas","a":"transaction-formulas","t":"The spot price tells you the current price per token, but you cannot simply multiply it by the quantity you want, because each token you buy slightly increases the price of the next one. Instead, the total cost is computed by integrating the spot price function across the full range of the transaction. The formulas below handle this automatically: two for buying (cost and tokens received) and one for selling (value received). Given a token quantity to acquire, this formula computes the total USDF cost by evaluating the definite integral from current supply to new supply: S₀, circulating supply before the purchase (currentSupply) S₁, circulating supply after the purchase (S₀ + tokensToBuy) Since b = c by design, the coefficient (a · b / c) simplifies to a curve.rs · tokens_to_value The computed cost always exceeds (spot price × quantity) because each successive token in the transaction is priced marginally higher than the previous. This price impact is an inherent property of pricing curves and scales with transaction size relative to total supply. When participants specify a USDF amount to spend (the typical case), this inverse function determines the exact quantity of MNY received"},{"f":"/docs/protocol/pricing-curve","p":"Pricing Curve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Onchain Implementation","a":"onchain","t":"All curve values are precomputed at fixed supply intervals and embedded in the program as lookup tables. Transaction pricing reduces to table lookups with flat step pricing, producing identical results every time. The implementation maintains two parallel data structures, each precomputed from the continuous formulas at fixed supply intervals: Discrete Pricing Table Spot price P(S) evaluated at each discrete supply level. Used for displaying current market price. Discrete Cumulative Value Table The integral of P(S) from 0 to each supply level. Enables efficient transaction cost computation by subtracting values at transaction boundaries. The lookup tables are configured to balance storage efficiency against pricing accuracy: ParameterValueNote Step size100 tokensEach entry is the curve value at S = i × 100; price is held constant within a step. Arithmetic128-bit · 18 dpFixed-point integers with 18-decimal scaling. The MNY token itself uses 10 decimals. Entries per table210,001Covers 0 → 21,000,000 MNY in 100-token increments. These tables are fully deterministic and independently verifiable. Any implementation can regenerate the complete table contents from the constants and formul"},{"f":"/docs/protocol/pricing-curve","p":"Pricing Curve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"Price Integrity and Secondary Markets","a":"price-integrity","t":"When price discovery is separated from actual ownership, price can move without any change in who holds the asset. Derivative markets allow leveraged participants to influence price without ever holding the underlying asset. Cascading liquidations and synthetic positions can drive price collapses that have no relationship to real demand or supply. The Reserve eliminates this class of vulnerability by design. Because the Reserve is the sole mechanism through which MNY enters and exits circulation, the Reserve price changes only when capital is committed or withdrawn. There are no external order books, no leveraged positions, and no derivative markets capable of influencing the Reserve's deterministic pricing. Secondary markets for MNY may exist, but they cannot alter the Reserve's deterministic pricing. Any divergence between secondary market prices and the Reserve creates an arbitrage opportunity that naturally drives convergence: Secondary Price > Reserve Price Arbitrageurs acquire MNY from the Reserve at the lower curve price, then sell on the secondary market at the elevated price. This increases circulating supply (raising the curve price) while adding sell pressure to the seco"},{"f":"/docs/protocol/pricing-curve","p":"Pricing Curve","g":"Moony Protocol","s":"","a":"","t":"The deterministic pricing curve that governs MNY."},{"f":"/docs/economy/overview","p":"Overview","g":"Moony Economy","s":"","a":"economy","t":"Moony is built to be a foundational asset for an open, composable economy. It carries value across the full range of economic activity, from everyday payments to programmable financial interactions, with no intermediary in the path. Open access and permissionless settlement let it serve as a shared medium of exchange wherever it is used. One open currency, from a cup of coffee to global capital markets."},{"f":"/docs/economy/overview","p":"Overview","g":"Moony Economy","s":"Built for economic activity","a":"why","t":"Four properties make Moony usable as money across vastly different scales, from a street vendor to a settlement layer, without changing form or asking permission. Instant settlement Transfers finalize onchain in under a second, around the clock. There is no clearing delay, no business-hours window, and no pending state to reconcile. Negligible fees Network costs are fractions of a cent, so value of any size moves economically, from a tip to a treasury transfer. Cost stops being a barrier to a transaction. Fine divisibility Ten decimal places make sub-cent pricing native, unlocking metering, streaming, and machine-to-machine value transfer that legacy rails cannot economically support. Composable & open As a standard token on an open program, Moony plugs into wallets, apps, and protocols without partnerships. Anyone can build on it, and those pieces compound."},{"f":"/docs/economy/overview","p":"Overview","g":"Moony Economy","s":"Economic Use Cases","a":"use-cases","t":"Direct value transfer between individuals, anywhere. Sub-cent transactions and programmable value streams. Composable finance built on open, permissionless rails."},{"f":"/docs/economy/overview","p":"Overview","g":"Moony Economy","s":"","a":"","t":"Moony as a foundational asset for an open, composable economy."},{"f":"/docs/economy/p2p","p":"P2P Payments","g":"Moony Economy","s":"","a":"p2p","t":"Peer-to-peer payments are Moony's core utility. By combining a fixed supply with fast, final onchain settlement, Moony enables permissionless, low-cost, and transparent value transfer between individuals without intermediaries."},{"f":"/docs/economy/p2p","p":"P2P Payments","g":"Moony Economy","s":"Why It Matters","a":"why-it-matters","t":"Legacy payment systems rely on layered intermediaries such as banks, processors, or card networks, each introducing cost, latency, and potential for control or exclusion. Transactions can be delayed, reversed, or denied entirely, with entire regions excluded from participation. Direct Settlement Transfers settle directly onchain between sender and recipient. No third party takes custody of funds at any point in the process, eliminating custodial counterparty risk. Censorship Resistance Valid transactions are processed by the network regardless of who initiates them. No central authority can selectively block, delay, or reverse transactions based on the identity of the parties, the nature of the transaction, or political considerations. Global Accessibility Anyone with internet access and a compatible wallet can participate. There are no geographic restrictions, account requirements, or identity verification barriers. The network operates 24/7, unaffected by weekends, holidays, or banking hours."},{"f":"/docs/economy/p2p","p":"P2P Payments","g":"Moony Economy","s":"Global Scalability","a":"scalability","t":"To make P2P viable at scale, both cost and throughput must support everyday usage. Moony operates on the Solana blockchain, making it suitable for daily transactions, not just high-value transfers. Throughput & Latency Solana processes thousands of transactions per second with sub-second finality, ensuring payments complete quickly even during peak usage. This makes Moony suitable for real-time payment scenarios where speed and reliability are critical. Low Fees Typical transfers cost fractions of a cent, with fees known at submission. This makes Moony economically viable as a medium of exchange for transactions of any size, removing cost as a barrier to everyday use. Energy Efficiency Solana's proof-of-stake consensus mechanism enables high throughput with minimal energy consumption per transaction. This efficiency ensures that Moony can scale to global adoption without the environmental costs associated with energy-intensive networks."},{"f":"/docs/economy/p2p","p":"P2P Payments","g":"Moony Economy","s":"Commerce","a":"commerce","t":"The same properties that make Moony effective for person-to-person transfers apply equally to commerce. Instant settlement, negligible fees, and permissionless access mean that any merchant, service, or platform can accept MNY without vendor agreements, processing intermediaries, or proprietary infrastructure. Adoption is not gated by institutional partnerships. It is driven by independent decisions to integrate an open standard. Digital Commerce Online merchants, applications, and digital services can accept MNY directly. Integration follows the same open token standards used across the Solana ecosystem, enabling checkout flows, subscription models, and in-app purchases without specialized payment infrastructure or third-party processors. Point of Sale MNY can be used for in-person transactions at physical merchants. Any device capable of generating or scanning a wallet address can serve as a point-of-sale terminal, enabling cash-like payments in the physical world with the same instant finality and zero fees as a digital transfer. Open Integration Because MNY follows standard token protocols on Solana, any developer can build payment acceptance into their application. There are n"},{"f":"/docs/economy/p2p","p":"P2P Payments","g":"Moony Economy","s":"Financial Inclusion","a":"inclusion","t":"Billions of people remain underserved or entirely excluded by traditional financial systems. In many regions, the absence of reliable banking infrastructure leaves individuals unable to send money across borders or participate in even the most basic forms of commerce. High inflation and economic instability further erode purchasing power, making it difficult to preserve wealth or plan for the future. Where banking does exist, high fees, slow settlement times, and rigid requirements place meaningful participation out of reach for many. Cross-border remittances can take days to arrive, cost double-digit percentages in fees, and are often impractical for small amounts, limiting economic mobility and reinforcing systemic barriers. Moony's peer-to-peer architecture offers a different path, one that provides access to a credibly scarce, sound digital currency while bypassing the bottlenecks of legacy finance. By removing intermediaries and leveraging low-cost, high-speed blockchain settlement, Moony extends open participation to anyone with a mobile device at a scale and inclusivity level traditional rails cannot match. Open Access Anyone with a smartphone can receive Moony without bank "},{"f":"/docs/economy/p2p","p":"P2P Payments","g":"Moony Economy","s":"Using it today","a":"interfaces","t":"Because the protocol is open, payments are not tied to any single app. The Flipcash mobile app is the simplest way to send and receive Moony right now, with familiar onramps and a polished experience, but it is one interface among many. Anyone can build a compatible wallet, point-of-sale tool, or application against the same Reserve, on the same terms."},{"f":"/docs/economy/p2p","p":"P2P Payments","g":"Moony Economy","s":"","a":"","t":"Peer-to-peer payments represent Moony's most direct utility."},{"f":"/docs/economy/micropayments","p":"Micropayments","g":"Moony Economy","s":"","a":"micro","t":"Micropayments are transactions valued in cents or fractions of a cent. Moony's extreme divisibility and near-zero transaction fees make them viable at global scale, unlocking entirely new economic behaviors that were previously impossible or uneconomical."},{"f":"/docs/economy/micropayments","p":"Micropayments","g":"Moony Economy","s":"Why It Matters","a":"why-it-matters","t":"The inability to move small amounts of value efficiently has shaped the digital economy in fundamental ways. Entire categories of commerce, monetization, and exchange remain unexplored because the cost of transacting exceeds the value being transferred. Micropayments are a missing building block of the modern internet economy. Subscriptions The impracticality of per-use billing forces consumers into subscription models, paying for more than they actually use. This all-or-nothing approach creates barriers to entry, limits access to content and services, and results in wasted spending on unused features or content. Exclusionary Entire categories of applications, such as real-time metering, per-unit API calls, or machine-to-machine commerce, remain underdeveloped because payment systems cannot handle the volume or economics they require. These use cases demand high-frequency, low-value transactions that traditional payment infrastructure cannot support economically."},{"f":"/docs/economy/micropayments","p":"Micropayments","g":"Moony Economy","s":"The Opportunity","a":"opportunity","t":"Moony is uniquely positioned to enable micropayments at scale. Its extreme divisibility enables transactions measured in fractions of a cent, and Solana's high throughput and near-zero fees ensure the cost of transacting remains negligible relative to the value being transferred. This is the foundation that makes real-time, granular monetization possible, enabling sustainable small-value transfers that expand markets, drive innovation, and open the digital economy to all. Pay-Per-Use Enable true pay-per-use access to content like articles, videos, AI queries, or software features without forcing users into recurring subscriptions. Creators gain new ways to monetize individual pieces of work while consumers pay only for what they use. Streaming Payments Transfer value continuously in real time for ongoing services like media consumption, cloud computing, or API access. Payment flows automatically match usage patterns, enabling dynamic billing models that only exist when the cost of each transaction is negligible. M2M Commerce Automate the exchange of resources like bandwidth, energy, data, or compute power in precise micro-increments between machines, devices, or services. With sub-"},{"f":"/docs/economy/micropayments","p":"Micropayments","g":"Moony Economy","s":"","a":"","t":"Enabling high-frequency, low-value transactions at scale."},{"f":"/docs/economy/capital","p":"Capital Markets","g":"Moony Economy","s":"","a":"capital","t":"Moony serves as a foundational digital asset for decentralized finance protocols. Its fixed supply, deterministic pricing curve, and onchain capital backing establish a transparent and verifiable basis for financial applications built on open, permissionless infrastructure."},{"f":"/docs/economy/capital","p":"Capital Markets","g":"Moony Economy","s":"Why It Matters","a":"why-it-matters","t":"The structure of traditional capital markets creates systemic dependencies on centralized intermediaries at every layer of participation. Access is gatekept, settlement is slow and costly, custody requires surrendering assets to institutions whose solvency cannot be independently verified, and price discovery remains vulnerable to manipulation through leveraged instruments detached from real ownership. Institutional Control Traditional capital markets require participants to meet institutional requirements to access financial products and to surrender custody of their assets once they do. Solvency and conduct of intermediaries cannot be independently verified. When those institutions fail, participants have limited recourse and consequences cascade across the system. Settlement Friction Traditional financial instruments settle over days through layered clearing processes. Each intermediary adds cost, latency, and counterparty risk. Markets operate on business hours, and cross-border settlement introduces further delays and fees. Market Manipulation In both traditional and decentralized markets, leveraged positions, synthetic exposure, and market-making structures allow price to mov"},{"f":"/docs/economy/capital","p":"Capital Markets","g":"Moony Economy","s":"The Opportunity","a":"opportunity","t":"Moony provides the infrastructure for a new class of decentralized financial applications. Open, permissionless access replaces institutional gatekeeping. Onchain settlement replaces multi-day clearing processes. Because every unit in circulation is backed by onchain capital with price determined deterministically by the Reserve, financial products built on MNY operate on a foundation of transparent, capital-backed pricing rather than external price discovery. Instant Settlement All transactions settle onchain with sub-second finality, 24/7, with no dependence on clearing houses, business hours, or intermediary processing. Settlement is final and irreversible at the moment of execution, eliminating the latency and counterparty risk inherent in multi-day clearing processes. Permissionless Exchange The Reserve provides a permissionless mechanism for buying and selling MNY directly onchain. Every purchase deposits capital into the Reserve, backing the circulating supply and enabling redemptions at price levels determined by the protocol's pricing curve. This liquidity is inherent to the protocol's design, present at every supply level regardless of external market conditions. Price In"},{"f":"/docs/economy/capital","p":"Capital Markets","g":"Moony Economy","s":"","a":"","t":"Moony integration into decentralized finance and capital markets."},{"f":"/docs/network/overview","p":"Overview","g":"Moony Network","s":"","a":"network","t":"The Moony Network is the community of independent participants who build, integrate, and transact with Moony. No company manages it, no governance body steers it, and no participant holds privileged access. It grows organically, as people choose to take part on their own terms, advancing Moony as a digital public good. No corporate management. No centralized governance. No privileged access."},{"f":"/docs/network/overview","p":"Overview","g":"Moony Network","s":"How an open network grows","a":"how-it-grows","t":"Without a company directing it, Moony spreads the way open standards do, through independent decisions that compound. Three dynamics drive that growth. Independent participation Anyone can build, integrate, or transact without coordination or approval. A developer ships a tool, a merchant accepts payment, a user transacts, each acting on their own initiative. Composability Each contribution is a building block others can extend. Tools, integrations, and applications stack and interoperate, so independent efforts reinforce rather than compete. Network effects More merchants attract more users, more users draw more builders, better tools attract more merchants. Adoption compounds on itself with no central party required."},{"f":"/docs/network/overview","p":"Overview","g":"Moony Network","s":"Network Structure","a":"structure","t":"The independent participants shaping the network. Aligned incentives that drive organic adoption."},{"f":"/docs/network/overview","p":"Overview","g":"Moony Network","s":"","a":"","t":"An open, permissionless network shaped by independent participation."},{"f":"/docs/network/stakeholders","p":"Stakeholders","g":"Moony Network","s":"Users & Community Members","a":"stakeholders","t":"Participants in the Moony Network emerge organically across a range of roles, motivations, and contributions. No central authority assigns or manages these roles. They arise independently through voluntary engagement with the protocol. Users & Community Members Users form the foundational layer of the Moony Network by sending, receiving, holding, and exchanging Moony in daily transactions. Through active usage, they provide real-world feedback, shape organic demand, and help guide network development. In a decentralized network, consistent usage itself constitutes meaningful participation. Merchants & Service Providers Merchants and service providers accept Moony as payment for goods and services across traditional retail, e-commerce, freelance work, content platforms, and informal economies. By bridging digital currency with real-world commerce, they validate Moony's practical utility and strengthen its legitimacy as functional money. Integrators & Infrastructure Partners Integrators, including payment platforms, custody solutions, on-ramps, and aggregators, embed Moony into existing financial and technical systems. This integration reduces friction and expands access, enabling se"},{"f":"/docs/network/stakeholders","p":"Stakeholders","g":"Moony Network","s":"How Participation Works","a":"how-participation-works","t":"The protocol's architecture allows participants to contribute without coordinating with a central team or gaining approval. The network's success depends on diverse actors building in divergent but interoperable ways, rather than seeking consensus from a core team. This absence of centralized control ensures that no single point of failure can limit innovation or restrict participation. Participation is composable: independent contributions stack and interoperate, creating compound effects that strengthen the entire system. This model produces decentralized resilience, layered utility, and a flywheel of mutually reinforcing adoption. Independent Contribution Participants can contribute at any level without approval or special access. A developer can build a payment integration, a creator can publish a tutorial, or a merchant can begin accepting Moony, all independently and in parallel. Innovation moves at the pace of independent actors, not centralized planning. Stackable Participation Contributions are inherently composable. A developer builds a tool, a merchant integrates it, users adopt it, and creators spread awareness, each layer strengthening the others. This modular design a"},{"f":"/docs/network/stakeholders","p":"Stakeholders","g":"Moony Network","s":"","a":"","t":"The independent participants who engage with the Moony protocol."},{"f":"/docs/network/incentives","p":"Incentives","g":"Moony Network","s":"","a":"incentives","t":"The Moony Network is designed to align individual initiative with collective network growth. As participants build, integrate, and transact with MNY, they expand its utility as a medium of exchange. With a fixed supply and transparent onchain reserve, increased utility drives demand through natural supply and demand dynamics, enabling participants to both strengthen and benefit from the network they help build."},{"f":"/docs/network/incentives","p":"Incentives","g":"Moony Network","s":"Recursive Flywheel","a":"flywheel","t":"These network effects compound through a recursive flywheel. Each contribution builds upon the last, creating a self-reinforcing cycle: individual actions increase utility, greater utility drives adoption, and broader adoption encourages more participation. This process gains momentum naturally as real usage and network strength grow together. An individual uses MNY for everyday transactions. A merchant accepts it as payment. A developer integrates it into a digital application. Each act of participation begins independently, driven by the utility Moony provides to that specific participant. Each new participant expands the practical ways to use Moony. Every transaction, merchant integration, and application built on MNY compounds the network's utility, extending its reach from peer-to-peer payments into commerce, financial products, and broader economic coordination. As usefulness expands, so does organic demand for Moony. Users need it to transact, merchants need it to accept payments, and developers need it to build and test applications. This demand arises naturally from real-world usage rather than speculative trading. Increased real-world demand is reflected in Moony's market"},{"f":"/docs/network/incentives","p":"Incentives","g":"Moony Network","s":"","a":"","t":"How the Moony network grows organically through participation."},{"f":"/docs/developers/architecture","p":"Architecture","g":"Developers","s":"Overview","a":"overview","t":"Moony runs on a single Solana program, the open-source Flipcash Reserve Contract. Moony is described entirely by a small set of onchain accounts: an SPL token mint, a currency configuration account, a liquidity pool, and two token vaults. Everything about Moony's behavior, its supply, its price, and its redemption liquidity, is a function of these accounts and the immutable code that operates on them. Program ID"},{"f":"/docs/developers/architecture","p":"Architecture","g":"Developers","s":"Account model","a":"accounts","t":"Two program-owned accounts hold a currency's state. Both are plain, fixed-layout structs, readable by anyone. Stores the currency's identity: its authority, mint, human-readable name and symbol, and the seed used to derive its PDAs. state/currency.rs MNY Token Mint The Reserve itself: it links the currency to its USDF base, tracks both vaults, accrued fees, and the sell-fee rate. The base mint is hardcoded to USDF. state/pool.rs Reserve (Pool) MNY Vault · vault_a USDF Vault · vault_b"},{"f":"/docs/developers/architecture","p":"Architecture","g":"Developers","s":"PDA derivation","a":"pdas","t":"Every account is a Program-Derived Address, deterministically derived from fixed seeds. Given a currency's authority, name, and seed, anyone can recompute the full account hierarchy offline, no indexer or lookup required."},{"f":"/docs/developers/architecture","p":"Architecture","g":"Developers","s":"Instruction set","a":"instructions","t":"These are the instructions that operate on Moony's Reserve. The trading instructions are open to anyone; burn_fees is restricted to the pool authority. InstructionPurposeAccess buyDeposit USDF, receive MNY at the curve price.Anyone sellReturn MNY, receive USDF less the sell fee.Anyone buy_and_deposit_into_vmBuy and deposit MNY into a Flipcash VM account in one transaction.Anyone sell_and_deposit_into_vmSell and deposit USDF proceeds into a VM account in one transaction.Anyone burn_feesBurn accumulated USDF fees from the pool vault; reset the counter.Authority"},{"f":"/docs/developers/architecture","p":"Architecture","g":"Developers","s":"Fixed parameters","a":"parameters","t":"Several values are compile-time constants in the program (api/src/consts.rs) that define Moony's hard limits. ConstantValue MAX_TOKEN_SUPPLY21,000,000 TOKEN_DECIMALS10 QUARKS_PER_TOKEN10,000,000,000 USDF_BASE_MINT5AMAA9JV…4RYAUQ"},{"f":"/docs/developers/architecture","p":"Architecture","g":"Developers","s":"","a":"","t":"How Moony is structured onchain: the accounts that hold its state, the PDAs that address them, and the instruction set that governs them."},{"f":"/docs/developers/cli","p":"Interacting with Moony via CLI","g":"Developers","s":"Introduction","a":"intro","t":"This page documents how to interact with Moony from the command line, using the CLI shipped with the open-source Flipcash Program. It covers querying the Reserve's onchain state, acquiring MNY with USDF, and redeeming MNY back to USDF, all by calling the Reserve contract directly. The CLI is part of the open-source release of the Flipcash Program; it was not built by Moony Labs and confers no privileged access. Because the Reserve is open, anyone can interact with it programmatically or build a compatible interface. The CLI is simply the reference tool for doing so from a terminal. It calls the same onchain Reserve under the same immutable rules, the same pricing curve, and the same hardcoded fees. If you're starting from scratch, this is the full sequence. Each step links to its section below. Install the prerequisites, Rust and the Solana CLI. Create and fund a keypair with a little SOL (for fees) and USDF (to spend). Build the flipcash-cli binary from source. Point every command at mainnet with --cluster m. Run get-currency first to confirm your setup works. It moves no funds. Buy a tiny test amount, verify it onchain, then transact for real."},{"f":"/docs/developers/cli","p":"Interacting with Moony via CLI","g":"Developers","s":"Prerequisites","a":"prerequisites","t":"Before you begin, make sure you have the following in place: Rust toolchain, rustc and cargo. Install via the official instructions at rust-lang.org. Solana CLI tools, install from the Solana documentation. A Solana keypair file, default location ~/.config/solana/id.json. A small amount of SOL in that keypair to pay transaction fees. USDF in the wallet if you intend to buy Moony, see Caveats & risks on acquiring USDF. Familiarity with command-line tools and basic Solana concepts. Confirm the toolchain is installed before going further: verify install If you don't already have a Solana keypair, create one. The CLI uses ~/.config/solana/id.json by default; pass --keypair <path> to use a different file. wallet setup Send a small amount of SOL to that address to cover transaction fees (a fraction of a SOL covers many transactions), plus the USDF you intend to spend. Both must be in the same keypair you sign with."},{"f":"/docs/developers/cli","p":"Interacting with Moony via CLI","g":"Developers","s":"Installation","a":"installation","t":"Clone the open-source repository and build the CLI binary from source: bash The compiled binary is written to ./target/release/flipcash-cli. Add it to your PATH, or invoke it with an absolute path. For additional installation notes, see the repository README."},{"f":"/docs/developers/cli","p":"Interacting with Moony via CLI","g":"Developers","s":"Cluster configuration","a":"cluster","t":"Moony lives on Solana mainnet. Every command must point there explicitly with --cluster m. The CLI defaults to localnet, which has no knowledge of Moony and will not work for real transactions. pattern You can also pass a custom RPC endpoint instead of the m shorthand if you run your own node or use a dedicated provider."},{"f":"/docs/developers/cli","p":"Interacting with Moony via CLI","g":"Developers","s":"Querying Moony's state get-currency","a":"query","t":"Returns Moony's current onchain state: the currency authority, mint, vaults, accumulated fees, and the sell-fee rate. Nothing is signed and no funds move. This is the safest command to run first to confirm your setup works. get-currency Use this to verify Moony's parameters or to read the current Reserve state before and after a transaction."},{"f":"/docs/developers/cli","p":"Interacting with Moony via CLI","g":"Developers","s":"Buying Moony buy","a":"buy","t":"Spends USDF to acquire MNY at the current curve position. The --amount flag specifies the USDF to spend, not the MNY to receive. The amount of MNY you get is determined by the curve at the moment the transaction executes. This command signs and submits a transaction with your keypair. buy Once the transaction confirms, MNY appears in your wallet. View it on the Solana Explorer using the returned signature (explorer.solana.com/tx/<signature>)."},{"f":"/docs/developers/cli","p":"Interacting with Moony via CLI","g":"Developers","s":"Selling Moony sell","a":"sell","t":"Returns MNY to the Reserve and receives USDF, less the 1% redemption fee. The --amount flag specifies the MNY to sell, not the USDF to receive. This command signs and submits a transaction with your keypair. sell The 1% fee is deducted from the USDF you receive. It is encoded in the Reserve and accrues to Flipcash Inc. Selling moves the curve down, the next buyer enters at a lower price. USDF is returned to your keypair's USDF token account."},{"f":"/docs/developers/cli","p":"Interacting with Moony via CLI","g":"Developers","s":"Verification","a":"verification","t":"After any transaction, confirm the result independently rather than trusting the CLI's output alone: Check the transaction confirmed on the Solana Explorer using the returned signature. Check your wallet's MNY balance, view the MNY token on Explorer. Confirm the Reserve state changed: run get-currency again and compare. Cross-reference with Solscan for human-readable token information."},{"f":"/docs/developers/cli","p":"Interacting with Moony via CLI","g":"Developers","s":"Caveats & risks","a":"caveats","t":"Always specify mainnet with --cluster m. Running on devnet or testnet will not interact with real Moony. The mint address must be exact: 4muAfB6m1P7C3Znad1VUsoqYFwvGQRkWGpJ3A4vupxz6. Any typo will either fail or interact with a different, or non-existent, currency. Keypair security: protect your keypair file. Anyone with access to it can spend your funds. USDF, specifically: you need USDF, not USDC, USDT, or another stablecoin. The CLI performs no automatic conversion. Acquiring USDF: most users obtain USDF through Flipcash's on-ramps. USDF is also available on Coinbase, and developers holding USDC can swap it for USDF via the Coinbase & Flipcash USDC↔USDF swap program."},{"f":"/docs/developers/cli","p":"Interacting with Moony via CLI","g":"Developers","s":"Reference addresses","a":"addresses","t":"The canonical onchain addresses, provided for verification. Each links to the Solana Explorer. AssetAddress MNY Token Mint 4muAfB6m1P7C3Znad1VUsoqYFwvGQRkWGpJ3A4vupxz6 Copy USDF Token Mint 5AMAA9JV9H97YYVxx8F6FsCMmTwXSuTTQneiup4RYAUQ Copy The Reserve (Pool) 5Ztd1ECKq4cXYt7BiRXK999hK2eQqtjJF9J3F1zw2jYC Copy Flipcash Program ccJYP5gjZqcEHaphcxAZvkxCrnTVfYMjyhSYkpQtf8Z Copy"},{"f":"/docs/developers/cli","p":"Interacting with Moony via CLI","g":"Developers","s":"Programmatic integration","a":"sdk","t":"For deeper integration, building an application rather than running CLI commands, the Flipcash Program exposes Rust instruction builders (api/src/sdk.rs) and a client crate. It is the same open path anyone can use to build wallets, exchanges, or other Moony-compatible interfaces. source Start from the source rather than this page; it is the authoritative reference for the program's instructions and accounts."},{"f":"/docs/developers/cli","p":"Interacting with Moony via CLI","g":"Developers","s":"Building your own interface","a":"interfaces","t":"The CLI is a thin wrapper over the Flipcash Program's instruction set. For deeper integration, a wallet, an exchange, a service, or any custom client, build against the program's instruction builders directly (api/src/sdk.rs and the client crate). They produce the identical Reserve instructions the CLI uses, so anything the CLI can do, your own code can too. The Reserve is open and there is no exclusivity: the CLI and any third-party client reach the same program on the same terms, the same curve, the same fees, the same immutable logic."},{"f":"/docs/developers/cli","p":"Interacting with Moony via CLI","g":"Developers","s":"Further reading","a":"further","t":""},{"f":"/docs/resources/branding","p":"Branding","g":"Resources","s":"The name","a":"name","t":"The name Moony combines moon and money, drawing from the aspirational language of internet culture while pointing directly to its function as a digital currency. It is intentionally memetic — simple, memorable, and shareable across diverse audiences and contexts. That accessibility is grounded by a brand and protocol rooted in credible public-good infrastructure."},{"f":"/docs/resources/branding","p":"Branding","g":"Resources","s":"The symbol","a":"symbol","t":"The Moony symbol is the Unicode character ⍜ U+235C · APL Functional Symbol Circle Underbar. The central form evokes a moon rising above a horizon, a direct reference to the name. The horizontal line anchors the mark; the ascending circle conveys aspiration and forward momentum. When rotated, the icon resembles a 1 and a 0, subtly invoking binary code. This visual echo underscores Moony's digital origin and its function as digital money."},{"f":"/docs/resources/branding","p":"Branding","g":"Resources","s":"Icon mark","a":"icon","t":"The standalone symbol, for avatars, favicons, and tight spaces. Use the variant with the strongest contrast against its background."},{"f":"/docs/resources/branding","p":"Branding","g":"Resources","s":"Wordmark","a":"wordmark","t":"The full moony wordmark, for headers, footers, and primary brand placements. Give it clear space and never stretch or recolor outside these variants."},{"f":"/docs/resources/branding","p":"Branding","g":"Resources","s":"Color palette","a":"color","t":"The lavender-to-cream gradient is Moony's signature, used sparingly as an accent — never as a flat background for body content."},{"f":"/docs/resources/branding","p":"Branding","g":"Resources","s":"Usage","a":"usage","t":"The Moony name, symbol, and visual assets are available for use by anyone building on, integrating with, or referencing the protocol. No permission is required. Developers, creators, merchants, and community members are encouraged to use these assets in their own applications, content, and documentation. Moony is a trademark of Moony Labs, LLC. The trademark exists to protect the network and its participants from misrepresentation or confusion, not to restrict legitimate use. If you're building in good faith, build freely."},{"f":"/docs/resources/branding","p":"Branding","g":"Resources","s":"","a":"","t":"The visual identity of the Moony protocol — its name, symbol, marks, and color. Open for anyone building on, integrating with, or referencing Moony."},{"f":"/docs/resources/disclaimer","p":"Disclaimer","g":"Legal","s":". General Notice","a":"general-notice","t":"This documentation is provided solely for informational and educational purposes. It does not constitute financial, investment, legal, tax, or other professional advice, nor should it be relied upon as such. Nothing contained herein should be interpreted as an offer, solicitation, recommendation, or endorsement to acquire, hold, use, or dispose of any digital asset, or to engage in any transaction or strategy. Interaction with blockchain-based systems is inherently experimental and voluntary. Any use of the Moony protocol or reliance on this documentation is undertaken entirely at the user's own risk. Users are responsible for conducting their own research and, where appropriate, consulting qualified professional advisors before engaging with decentralized technologies."},{"f":"/docs/resources/disclaimer","p":"Disclaimer","g":"Legal","s":". Moony Protocol Overview","a":"protocol-overview","t":"Moony is a decentralized digital currency governed by an immutable program on the Solana blockchain. Once created onchain, the protocol operates autonomously according to its code. It is permissionless and self-executing, and does not depend on any company, organization, or individual for its ongoing operation. No representation is made that the protocol will function as intended in all circumstances, maintain any particular value, or achieve any specific outcome. The protocol's behavior is determined solely by its onchain logic and the actions of independent network participants."},{"f":"/docs/resources/disclaimer","p":"Disclaimer","g":"Legal","s":". Role of Moony Labs LLC","a":"role-of-labs","t":"Moony Labs LLC served a narrowly defined role in the initial creation of the Moony protocol. That role was limited to creating the protocol onchain and making related documentation publicly available. Moony Labs does not own, operate, or control the protocol following creation. It does not retain discretionary control over token supply, protocol pricing, redemption mechanics, or network participation. There are no upgrade mechanisms, pause mechanisms, or backdoors. Moony Labs does not receive proceeds from the protocol, does not participate in reserve flows, and does not hold any special economic position relative to other participants. Moony was created without venture financing, token pre-sales, or investor-linked performance expectations. No ongoing development, maintenance, promotion, or stewardship is promised or implied. The protocol is not dependent on continued involvement by Moony Labs to exist or function. This limited role is intentional. The protocol is designed to exist as neutral public infrastructure, shaped by open participation rather than centralized coordination."},{"f":"/docs/resources/disclaimer","p":"Disclaimer","g":"Legal","s":". Risks, No Warranties, and Limitation of Liability","a":"risks","t":"Blockchain technologies involve material risks, including but not limited to market volatility, program vulnerabilities, network congestion or outages, cybersecurity incidents, irreversible transactions, and the potential for total loss of digital assets. The Moony protocol and all related documentation are provided on an \"as is\" and \"as available\" basis. Moony Labs LLC makes no representations or warranties of any kind, whether express or implied, including warranties of merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose, accuracy, security, or non-infringement. To the maximum extent permitted by applicable law, Moony Labs LLC disclaims all liability for any direct or indirect losses or damages arising from use of the protocol, reliance on this documentation, or interaction with blockchain systems."},{"f":"/docs/resources/disclaimer","p":"Disclaimer","g":"Legal","s":". Open-Source References","a":"open-source-references","t":"For transparency and reproducibility, this site references public repositories and technical materials associated with the original creation of the protocol. These materials are provided as open reference resources for developers, researchers, and other interested parties. Where applicable, such materials are governed by their respective open-source licenses. Moony Labs does not claim proprietary rights over third-party software and does not restrict lawful use, modification, or redistribution of such materials in accordance with those licenses."},{"f":"/docs/resources/disclaimer","p":"Disclaimer","g":"Legal","s":". Regulatory Considerations","a":"regulatory","t":"Nothing in this documentation should be interpreted as creating, implying, or guaranteeing any regulatory status, classification, or compliance outcome for the Moony protocol or any associated digital asset. Regulatory treatment of blockchain technologies varies across jurisdictions and may change over time. Users are solely responsible for understanding and complying with all applicable laws and regulations relevant to their activities."},{"f":"/docs/resources/disclaimer","p":"Disclaimer","g":"Legal","s":". Updates","a":"updates","t":"This disclaimer may be updated from time to time to reflect clarifications or changes in context. Updates will be posted on this page with a revised date. Last updated: April 2026"},{"f":"/docs/resources/disclaimer","p":"Disclaimer","g":"Legal","s":"","a":"","t":"This documentation is provided solely for informational and educational purposes. Interaction with blockchain-based systems is experimental and undertaken entirely at your own risk."}];
